1.1.text:
Article 3

(1) The purpose of implementing protection of women and children from acts of violence is to:

a. prevent acts of violence against women and children including trafficking in persons;
b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children:
c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
d. provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. facilitate and mediate domestic disputes to realize the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.
(2) The purpose of implementing protection of women and children from acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), includes aspects of:
a. prevention;
b. service and assistance;
c. reunification; and
d. empowerment.

Article 10

(1) The Local Government has the duty to implement efforts to prevent violence against women and children, in the form of:

a. collecting data and information on women and children victims of acts of violence, and laws and regulations;
b. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence;
c. conducting education on anti-violence values towards women and children; and
d. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence.
(2) The Local Government in anticipating acts of violence against women and children, is authorized to provide and implement services for victims in the form of:
a. providing and facilitating the establishment of integrated services for victims of acts of violence involving institutions and community elements;
b. encouraging community awareness of the importance of protection for victims; and
c. conducting community empowerment for the prevention of acts of violence.

Article 17

(1) To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the regional government conducts empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.

(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government undertakes efforts as follows:

a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Article 2

The implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence is carried out based on the principles of:

a. respect for the rights of victims;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination; and
d. best interests of victims.

Article 25

(1) In order to achieve the objectives of providing protection to women and children from acts of violence as referred to in Article 3, the regional government cooperates with:

a. the government;
b. the provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments; and
d. non-governmental organizations.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes: a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of acts of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses, and followed up in accordance with applicable law.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is set forth in the form of a joint agreement.

Article 27

(1) The Regional Government is obliged to conduct guidance and supervision of the provision of protection to women and children from acts of violence.

(2) Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1), includes: a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;

b. technical guidance and training;

c. provision of facilities;

d. monitoring; and

e. evaluation.

(3) Supervision as referred to in paragraph (1), is conducted with the principles:

a. professional;
b. transparent; and
c. accountable.

Article 34

(1) In addition to general investigation officials, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation, may also be conducted by civil servant investigators as referred to in the Criminal

Procedure Code.

(2) Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1), are certain civil servant officials within the regional government who are appointed by authorized officials in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations

(3) In carrying out investigation duties, the investigation officials as referred to in paragraph (1), are authorized:

a. to receive reports or complaints from a person regarding criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. to take initial action at that time at the scene and conduct examination;
c. to interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identification;
d. to seize objects and/or letters;
e. to take fingerprints and photograph a person;
f. to summon people to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. to bring in experts needed in connection with case examination;
h. to terminate investigation after receiving instructions from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify the public prosecutor, suspect or their family; and/or
i. to take other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be accounted for.
(4) Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1), shall notify the commencement of investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the public prosecutor through investigators of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Code.

REGIONAL REGULATION OF MATARAM CITY

REGIONAL REGULATION OF MATARAM CITY

NUMBER 4 OF 2012

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

MAYOR OF MATARAM,

Considering : a. that violence against women and children is an act that violates the rights and dignity of women and children as human beings;

b. that the implementation of protection of women and children from acts of violence has not been carried out optimally;

c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a and b, it is necessary to enact a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection of Women and Children from Acts of Violence;

In view of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 4 of 1993 concerning the Establishment of the Municipality of Second Level Region of Mataram (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1993 Number 66, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3531);

3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);

4. Law Number 23 of 2004 Concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);

5. Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 125, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4437) as amended several times, most recently by Law Number 12 of 2008 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 32 of 2004 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 59, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4844);

6. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning the Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);

7. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Criminal Offense of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);

8. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);

9. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning the Implementation of Women's Protection;

10. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 Concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;

11. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 1 of 2010 concerning Minimum Service Standards for Integrated Services for Women and Child Victims of Violence;

12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers;

13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence;

14. Regional Regulation of the Province of West Nusa Tenggara Number 2 of 2009 concerning the Implementation of Prevention and Protection of Women and Child Victims of Acts of Violence (Regional Gazette of the Province of West Nusa Tenggara of 2009 Number 15, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of the Province of West Nusa Tenggara Number 45);

With Joint Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF MATARAM CITY

and

MAYOR OF MATARAM

HAS DECIDED:

To enact : REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN FROM ACTS OF VIOLENCE.

Article 18

(1) Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 17, is implemented by SKPD and UKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of: a. social;

b. health;

c. education;

d. manpower;

e. women's empowerment and child protection; f. mental and spiritual; and

g. peace and order.

(2) Prevention of acts of violence by SKPD and UKPD as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 23

(1) The Regional Government is obliged to conduct empowerment of women victims of acts of violence.

(2) Empowerment of victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented by P2TP2A.

(3) In conducting empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government prepares an empowerment program in the economic field, which includes:

a. procuring the needs required for entrepreneurship training for women victims of acts of violence, in order to improve knowledge, attitude, and business skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various work training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic enterprises;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large companies, as well as financial institutions to develop businesses of women victims of acts of violence;
e. seeking the provision of capital for women victims of acts of violence; and
f. expanding access to information and promoting the products of women victims of acts of violence.
(4) In conducting empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government may establish cooperation with job training institutions organized by the private sector.

Article 21

(1) Forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence, are as follows:

a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. repatriation;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), are implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by SKPD and UKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:
a. social;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and d. mental and spiritual.
(3) In the implementation of duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the regional government cooperates with government agencies, provincial government, other regency/city governments, and the community.
(4) Further provisions regarding the procedures for services, and handling of women and children victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3), are regulated by Mayor Regulation.

