Article 23

(1) The Regional Government is obliged to conduct empowerment of women victims of acts of violence.

(2) Empowerment of victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), is implemented by P2TP2A.

(3) In conducting empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government prepares an empowerment program in the economic field, which includes:

a. procuring the needs required for entrepreneurship training for women victims of acts of violence, in order to improve knowledge, attitude, and business skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various work training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic enterprises;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large companies, as well as financial institutions to develop businesses of women victims of acts of violence;
e. seeking the provision of capital for women victims of acts of violence; and
f. expanding access to information and promoting the products of women victims of acts of violence.
(4) In conducting empowerment of women victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government may establish cooperation with job training institutions organized by the private sector.

Article 17

(1) To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the regional government conducts empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.

(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the regional government undertakes efforts as follows:

a. increasing the quantity and quality of education both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; and
g. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Article 21

(1) Forms of services provided to women and children victims of acts of violence, are as follows:

a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. legal aid;
d. repatriation;
e. rehabilitation, social reintegration, and medicolegal;
f. identification services; and
g. psychological services.
(2) Forms of services as referred to in paragraph (1), are implemented in accordance with minimum service standards established by the government and implemented by SKPD and UKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:
a. social;
b. health;
c. women's empowerment and child protection; and d. mental and spiritual.
(3) In the implementation of duties and functions as referred to in paragraph (2), the regional government cooperates with government agencies, provincial government, other regency/city governments, and the community.
(4) Further provisions regarding the procedures for services, and handling of women and children victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), and paragraph (3), are regulated by Mayor Regulation.

